  "evidence": "All published ZIPs have matching registry byte lengths and MD5; all embedded measurement CSV/XLSX files processed using their actual headers and contents. Experiment scenarios in the registry describe the use case. Vehicle commands, motor actuation or a guaranteed sensed close-loop response are not exposed by these network aggregates.",
  "results": [
    "62 ZIPs, 130 CSV member occurrences, 94 unique SHA-256 payloads. Of the 62 XML records two declare QC_ERROR, three have end earlier than start. The per-file table includes all versions and marks rows with mismatched widths or nonnumeric time.",
    "After removing byte-identical members, in the application files 131,324 rows are readable; collapsing identical complete trimmed text rows separately within each distinct file leaves 43,960. There are 3,221 within-file timestamp/endpoint/protocol/direction/radio/message keys with conflicting (tx,rx,latency) tuples. Separate distinct network file payloads contain 63,307 data lines failing the declared schema width or timestamp. No attempt made to repair shifts caused by a missing column.",
    "Neither row multiplicity nor warning-free parsing establishes independent transmissions. Released observation-level counters and aggregate latency lack individual message IDs and application-to-servo timing. Some headerless application/access/event files were parsed only with a template verified in headed files, flagged individually."
